                              Brute Force
class Solution {
    public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] ans = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int product = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (i != j) {
                    product *= nums[j];
                }
            }
            ans[i] = product;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

                              Intuition
Instead of recomputing the product for every index, we split the work into two passes.

First pass (Left to Right): Store the product of all elements to the left of each index in the answer array.
Second pass (Right to Left): Maintain the product of all elements to the right of each index and multiply it with the already stored left product.
Thus, each position gets:
Answer[i]=(Product of Left Elements)×(Product of Right Elements)

                          Time Compleity
Time Complexity: O(n)
